--- A collecting `for` whose item is an empty list held in a local (`none = []`,
--- then `none`), its items later given ints (`adj[1] = push[adj[1], 7]`):
--- the interpreter runs it, the compiled program does not build.
---
--- crow run -i:   [[], [7], []]
--- crow run:      error[E0308]: mismatched types
---                  --> src/main.rs:37:34
---                error: the compiled program does not build — the Rust compiler
---                refused what Crow accepted, a bug in Crow, not in this program
--- The generated Rust declares the local as `let none: Vec<String> = vec![];` and
--- collects `Vec<Vec<String>>` into a variable typed `Vec<Vec<i64>>`.
--- Expected: [[], [7], []] in both modes. Writing `[]` itself as the body's last
--- line (no local), or the literal `[[], [], []]`, builds and agrees. The same
--- inside a function (adjacency lists of a graph, built per vertex then pushed).
--- Found writing rosetta/hopcroft_karp_algorithm.co.

adj = for u in range[3]
    none = []
    none
adj[1] = push[adj[1], 7]
log[adj]
